{
  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Detroit Tigers opened the year with a jolt of bad news: Tarik Skubal, their ace and reigning Cy Young Award winner, was tagged for a three-run homer by Teoscar Hernández on Opening Day, setting an anxious tone. But the Tigers responded the very next night when catcher Dillon Dingler laced a go-ahead triple in one of the season's earliest clutch moments, signaling that this club had the fight to answer adversity. Through the early weeks, the offense and rotation took turns lifting and sinking the team, with promising contributions from Spencer Torkelson and the bullpen already showing cracks.\n\nThe month of May became the season's emotional centerpiece. Javier Báez, whose career had seemed to be fading, erupted in a stunning one-game showcase against Boston on May 13th, clubbing two home runs — including a go-ahead three-run blast that swung nearly two-thirds of a win in Detroit's favor. Trey Sweeney added a clutch single in the same game, and the Tigers rallied again the next day on a Justyn-Henry Malloy single. That stretch planted a belief in the clubhouse that this team could compete deep into October.\n\nThe back half of the regular season was a grind defined by rotating heroes and persistent bullpen troubles. Riley Greene delivered a grand slam in late August to answer a grand slam Skubal had surrendered the night before. Kerry Carpenter blossomed into a reliable offensive threat, punctuating a September push with his 26th home run. But Will Vest became a recurring source of heartbreak, with multiple late-season blown leads — including back-to-back damaging singles on September 20th — nearly costing Detroit its playoff berth. The Tigers steadied themselves at 87-75, good enough for the postseason.\n\nDetroit's October run was brief but charged with drama. A wild September 30th Wild Card moment — José Ramírez gunned down at the plate on a fielder's choice — gave the Tigers a lifeline they ultimately parlayed into advancement. In the ALDS against Seattle, Kerry Carpenter homered again and Spencer Torkelson drove in runs in back-to-back games, and Javier Báez's defense sparkled in a clinching double play. But the Guardians and Mariners had too much firepower, and Detroit's season ended in the Division Series, a promising, prideful campaign that fell one round short of the promised land.",
  "chapters": [
